        "text": ": 1. The Orrin Valley network runs 214 kilometres of double track between the port of Calder Reach and the upland towns around Mirror Lake. Every one of its 61 electric trainsets carries roughly 1,900 sensors: axle-box thermometers, pantograph force gauges, door controllers, traction inverter monitors, brake cylinder pressure transducers and a few dozen accelerometers bolted to the bogie frames. Until the last refit, the readings were written to removable flash cards that depot staff collected once a week. A failing bearing therefore announced itself, at best, seven days after the first warning sign, and twice in that period the first sign anyone noticed was smoke.\n\nTessaline is the storage service that replaced the flash cards. It receives a continuous stream from every trainset over the cellular modems fitted during the refit, keeps eighteen months of full-resolution history online, and answers the queries that maintenance planners, the control room and two external research partners send it. This document describes how the service is built, why the main decisions were made, what has gone wrong in its first two years of operation, and which problems remain open. It is written for engineers who will change the system, so it spends more time on trade-offs and failure behaviour than",
